This motivates our central questions:
Are foundation models that are good at building agents also good at being agents?
Our benchmark:
 To address this gap, we introduce EvolvingBench, where each evaluation pairs a builder/evolver model with a solver/agent model on an existing agentic benchmark. The solver first interacts with experience tasks using an initial agent harness, producing trajectories, failures, and feedback. The builder then updates the harness under a fixed evolution budget. At the end of each run, the solver is evaluated on held-out tasks using the evolved harness.
We enforce the minimal constraints necessary to preserve evaluation integrity: the builder may not access held-out evaluation tasks, may not modify the evaluation harness, may not update solver weights, and may only improve the agent through harness updates such as prompts, skills, memory, tool notes, or workflows.
The goal is not to maximize one agent’s final score alone, but to decompose self-evolution performance into four components: the builder model’s ability to improve agents, the solver model’s ability to benefit from the evolved harness, domain effects, and builder–solver interaction.

