Why this is a benchmark
EvolvingBench is a benchmark not because it introduces a new task dataset, but because it defines a repeatable, comparable, and constrained evaluation protocol for a capability that existing benchmarks do not isolate.
A benchmark needs to define what is evaluated, under what conditions, what is allowed, what is scored, and how results are compared. EvolvingBench does this through six components:.

Our benchmark has 6 factors:
Evaluation object
我们不是泛泛评估 “A-Evolve 效果”，而是评估一个 self-evolution cell：
builder/evolver model × solver/agent model × benchmark domain。
Standardized input
如果最后是train/test split, 我们可以说: 每个 cell 都有相同的 initial harness、experience task set、held-out evaluation set、evolution protocol 和 budget。
如果是in-situ, 我们可以说：fixed task stream, fixed initial harness, fixed evolution protocol, fixed budget, fixed feedback format.
Standardized input
每个 cell 都有相同类型的 initial harness、experience tasks、held-out eval tasks、budget 和 protocol。
Controlled autonomy
如果是train/test split, 我们可以说：evolver 可以更新 harness，但不能看 held-out test、不能改 eval harness、不能改 solver weights。这保证 evaluation integrity，避免变成作弊或不可控的 open-ended experiment。
如果是in-situ, 我们可以说：evolver 可以根据过去任务更新 harness，但不能看未来任务，不能改 solver weights，不能改 evaluation script。
Standardized output
输出的是 evolved harness，而不是任意 artifact。这让不同 runs 的结果可以被统一记录、比较和分析。
Scoring
如果是train/test split, 我们可以说：最终只在 held-out tasks 上测 solver + evolved harness。这保证我们测的是 generalizable improvement，而不是在 experience tasks 上 overfit。
如果是in-situ, 我们可以说：we use online / in-situ score：
each task is scored before its feedback can be used for future updates.
Metrics
不只报告 final score，还报告 builder-marginal、solver-marginal、role correlation、variance decomposition。
这让 benchmark 能回答我们的核心问题：gain 到底来自 builder、solver、domain，还是 interaction。


In short:
PostTrainBench benchmarks whether agents can improve model weights; our benchmark measures whether foundation models can improve and use agent harnesses.


Benchmark Setup
We use an in-situ self-evolution protocol. For each benchmark domain (b), we define:

a task stream (D_b = (x_1, x_2, \dots, x_T));
an initial harness (H_b^0);
a builder/evolver model (e);
a solver/agent model (s);
a fixed evolution budget (\beta).

At step (t), the solver acts on task (x_t) using the current harness:

[ (a_t, \tau_t) = \mathrm{Solve}(A(s, H_{e,s,b}^{t-1}), x_t) ]

The task is scored immediately:

[ r_t = \mathrm{Score}(a_t, x_t) ]

Only after the task is scored can the builder observe the trajectory and feedback and update the harness for future tasks:

[ H_{e,s,b}^{t} = U_e(H_{e,s,b}^{t-1}, \tau_t, r_t; \beta) ]

The in-situ self-evolution score is:

[ S(e,s,b) = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} r_t ]

The no-evolution baseline is:

[ S_0(s,b) = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathrm{Score}(\mathrm{Solve}(A(s,H_b^0),x_t),x_t) ]

The improvement is:

[ \Delta(e,s,b)=S(e,s,b)-S_0(s,b) ]

We then compute role-marginal capability:

同一evolver在不同domain，不同solver下的average performance [ \mathrm{BuilderMarginal}(e)=\mathbb{E}_{s,b}[\Delta(e,s,b)] ]

同solver在不同domain，不evolver下的average performance [ \mathrm{SolverMarginal}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{e,b}[\Delta(e,s,b)] ]

Important clarification:

We fix the task stream, initial harness, evolution protocol, budget, builder model, and solver model for each cell. We do not fix the resulting trajectories, because trajectories are generated by the solver interacting with the current harness and environment. This is intentional: in closed-loop self-evolution, the solver’s ability to expose informative failures is part of its agent-side capability.

